00:00:08.809 --> 00:00:15.050
The United States, despite its geopolitical
prominence, is flanked by but two countries.

00:00:15.050 --> 00:00:22.289
To the north, the 5,525 mile, 8,891 kilometer
line separating it from Canada represents

00:00:22.289 --> 00:00:24.509
the longest border in the world.

00:00:24.509 --> 00:00:32.009
To the south, the 1,954 mile, 3,145 kilometer
US-Mexico border is perhaps one of the most

00:00:32.009 --> 00:00:34.210
significant in the world.

00:00:34.210 --> 00:00:38.350
It separates two of the most economically
and socially interlinked countries in the

00:00:38.350 --> 00:00:39.350
world.

00:00:39.350 --> 00:00:43.879
The US buys more from Mexico than any other
country, Mexico buys more from the US than

00:00:43.879 --> 00:00:47.920
any other country, and Mexico sells more to
the US than any other country.

00:00:47.920 --> 00:00:53.829
Beyond that, there are some 37 million people
living in the US with Mexican ancestry, which

00:00:53.829 --> 00:00:56.429
is a truly staggering statistic.

00:00:56.429 --> 00:01:00.170
That means there are more Mexicans living
in the US than there are people living in

00:01:00.170 --> 00:01:02.340
Canada, full stop.

00:01:02.340 --> 00:01:07.530
Both the US and Mexico rely hugely on this
continuous flow of people and money back and

00:01:07.530 --> 00:01:13.500
forth, which means how well this border functions
is directly tied to the success of both countries.

00:01:13.500 --> 00:01:19.030
Unfortunately for both, though, this border,
while significant, has its issues.

00:01:19.030 --> 00:01:23.549
A border and its effectiveness involves far
more than the line itself—it’s about how

00:01:23.549 --> 00:01:27.720
well the overall immigration system between
the two countries functions—but, at the

00:01:27.720 --> 00:01:31.360
end of the day, that line is still quite important.

00:01:31.360 --> 00:01:36.410
The Mexico-US border is crossed more than
any other restricted border in the world,

00:01:36.410 --> 00:01:38.909
meaning it’s actually quite porous.

00:01:38.909 --> 00:01:42.340
There are fifty spots where one can legally
cross the border by land.

00:01:42.340 --> 00:01:47.369
The smallest is the tiny Boquillas Port of
Entry in Big Bend National Park where about

00:01:47.369 --> 00:01:52.829
11,000 annual travelers are processed remotely
using video inspection kiosks—mostly to

00:01:52.829 --> 00:01:56.570
make day-trips to the small Mexican town of
Boquillas del Carmen while vacationing in

00:01:56.570 --> 00:01:57.759
the National Park.

00:01:57.759 --> 00:02:02.909
On the other hand, the largest crossing is
the San Ysidro Port of Entry, processing 11

00:02:02.909 --> 00:02:07.869
million pedestrians and 26 million cars per
year—thereby making it the busiest land

00:02:07.869 --> 00:02:10.800
border checkpoint in the western hemisphere.

00:02:10.800 --> 00:02:15.190
As part of the primary route between San Diego
and Tijuana—two cities between which people

00:02:15.190 --> 00:02:19.660
commute on a daily basis—this crossing is
never not busy.

00:02:19.660 --> 00:02:27.710
The rest of this 1,954 mile, 3,145 kilometer
line, beyond these 50 checkpoints, is secured

00:02:27.710 --> 00:02:32.870
as, to go from Mexico to the US, or the US
to Mexico, you have to go through immigration

00:02:32.870 --> 00:02:36.560
checkpoints—at least to do it legally.

00:02:36.560 --> 00:02:40.940
Each and every year, hundreds of thousands
or millions of people attempt to cross into

00:02:40.940 --> 00:02:45.590
the US, via the southern border, without the
legal right to do so.

00:02:45.590 --> 00:02:48.450
In short, they try to migrate illegally.

00:02:48.450 --> 00:02:53.980
Now, the vast majority of these undocumented
immigrants are not successful in their endeavor—an

00:02:53.980 --> 00:02:59.070
estimated 55 to 85 percent of them are caught
and sent back to their home country—but

00:02:59.070 --> 00:03:02.200
to many people, these odds are worth it.

00:03:02.200 --> 00:03:07.720
There is enormous economic disparity between
the US, where average income is at $65,000

00:03:07.720 --> 00:03:13.350
per year, and Mexico, where that number stands
at $9,500 per year.

00:03:13.350 --> 00:03:17.520
Also, while low compared to other wealthy
countries, the US scores much better than

00:03:17.520 --> 00:03:22.410
Mexico on social mobility meaning a poor person
is more likely to become wealthier in their

00:03:22.410 --> 00:03:24.990
lifetime in the US than Mexico.

00:03:24.990 --> 00:03:28.500
In short, while there are certainly nearly
infinite reasons why someone might choose

00:03:28.500 --> 00:03:34.050
to migrate to the US—including, quite often,
family reunification or an escape from violence—research

00:03:34.050 --> 00:03:39.240
has shown that money, and the attempt to escape
poverty, is typically the strongest and primary

00:03:39.240 --> 00:03:40.240
one.

00:03:40.240 --> 00:03:44.790
Of course, the question is, why don’t these
people migrate to the US legally?

00:03:44.790 --> 00:03:47.060
It would certainly be safer and cheaper.

00:03:47.060 --> 00:03:51.580
Well, overwhelmingly the answer is because
they can’t.

00:03:51.580 --> 00:03:57.230
For some people, there is no legitimate route
to legal status in the US, and, to others,

00:03:57.230 --> 00:03:59.180
there’s no practical way.

00:03:59.180 --> 00:04:04.250
Many forms of American immigrant visas have
annual caps that are not proportional to immigration

00:04:04.250 --> 00:04:06.210
demand from a given country.

00:04:06.210 --> 00:04:10.670
That means that, for example, if you were
a married, Mexican-citizen who was the son

00:04:10.670 --> 00:04:15.570
or daughter of a US citizen, the wait time
to have your visa processed currently stands

00:04:15.570 --> 00:04:18.220
at twenty-four years.

00:04:18.220 --> 00:04:23.400
That means that now, in September 2020, they’re
currently only processing applications submitted

00:04:23.400 --> 00:04:26.520
up until August 1st, 1996.

00:04:26.520 --> 00:04:30.780
Faced with the prospect of waiting 24 years,
more than a fourth of their life, until they

00:04:30.780 --> 00:04:35.009
can live and work in the same country as their
parents or, for example, take care of them

00:04:35.009 --> 00:04:40.539
in old age, many people seeking this and other
classes of visas choose not to wait, and rather

00:04:40.539 --> 00:04:46.639
take the faster, yet riskier option of immigrating
without authorization.

00:04:46.639 --> 00:04:51.490
While dangerous, the process of crossing the
border illegally is a path generally not taken

00:04:51.490 --> 00:04:52.729
alone.

00:04:52.729 --> 00:04:57.180
Back twenty years ago, people had to figure
out this process themselves, but nowadays,

00:04:57.180 --> 00:05:02.159
upwards of 95% of first time crossers hire
a smuggler to help them.

00:05:02.159 --> 00:05:07.479
That’s an expensive proposition, with the
average fee hovering around $3,500 per person—about

00:05:07.479 --> 00:05:10.430
four month’s wages for the average Mexican.

00:05:10.430 --> 00:05:14.729
In exchange for their fees, these smugglers
will generally group migrants together, transport

00:05:14.729 --> 00:05:18.570
them to the border, give them instructions
on how to cross, and then arrange for transportation

00:05:18.570 --> 00:05:20.120
on the other side.

00:05:20.120 --> 00:05:25.180
The migrants and smugglers really have two
choices in terms of which obstacle to face—the

00:05:25.180 --> 00:05:28.120
border security itself, or nature.

00:05:28.120 --> 00:05:33.099
There is never no security, but in many places
there is minimal as hundreds of miles of empty,

00:05:33.099 --> 00:05:37.749
dry, desert plains lay between the border
and the nearest city, where migrants can disappear

00:05:37.749 --> 00:05:39.240
into the crowd.

00:05:39.240 --> 00:05:44.460
This sheer isolation is a big reason why there
is no physical man-made barrier on the border

00:05:44.460 --> 00:05:49.770
in almost all of Texas, and across many other
stretches in New Mexico, Arizona, and California.

00:05:49.770 --> 00:05:55.099
It's tough to get an exact figure, as it’s
ever changing and poorly recorded, but currently,

00:05:55.099 --> 00:05:58.449
only about a third of the border is secured
with a barrier.

00:05:58.449 --> 00:06:03.389
In fact, the majority of that third takes
the form of a vehicle barrier, intended only

00:06:03.389 --> 00:06:07.759
to stop a car or truck from driving across
the border, rather than a full pedestrian

00:06:07.759 --> 00:06:09.300
barrier.

00:06:09.300 --> 00:06:13.690
Generally there’s only a pedestrian barrier
in heavily populated areas like Brownsville,

00:06:13.690 --> 00:06:16.260
El Paso, Yuma, and San Diego.

00:06:16.260 --> 00:06:21.199
Even in these populated areas, one doesn't
have to go far to find a gap in the wall.

00:06:21.199 --> 00:06:24.729
Starting from the western end of the border
at the Pacific Ocean, the first wall-less

00:06:24.729 --> 00:06:29.999
section appears less than 15 miles or 23 kilometers
to the east, at the first section of steep

00:06:29.999 --> 00:06:31.150
terrain.

00:06:31.150 --> 00:06:35.479
Any barrier, though, doesn’t tend to be
too much of an issue for smugglers.

00:06:35.479 --> 00:06:40.439
Often, they’ll have scouts on both sides,
wait for border patrol to leave the area,

00:06:40.439 --> 00:06:44.040
then install a ladder and quickly climb over.

00:06:44.040 --> 00:06:48.249
Cartels have been known to go even a step
further and build tunnels under the border.

00:06:48.249 --> 00:06:54.460
In January, 2020, US Customs and Border Protection
discovered the longest ever—a 4,300 foot,

00:06:54.460 --> 00:06:58.360
1,300 meter structure stretching from Tijuana
to San Diego.

00:06:58.360 --> 00:07:03.129
This facility was so advanced that it had
ventilation, electricity, a rail system, and

00:07:03.129 --> 00:07:04.819
even an elevator.

00:07:04.819 --> 00:07:09.680
While its unclear exactly who built and operated
this tunnel, in the past, many of these have

00:07:09.680 --> 00:07:14.320
been linked to large crime syndicates like
the Sinaloa Cartel, demonstrating the unintended

00:07:14.320 --> 00:07:17.380
consequences that securing the border can
have.

00:07:17.380 --> 00:07:21.710
Demand for hard drugs is fairly inelastic,
meaning people who consume them are going

00:07:21.710 --> 00:07:24.080
to buy them no matter the cost.

00:07:24.080 --> 00:07:27.620
As it gets harder and harder for drugs and
other controlled substances to be smuggled

00:07:27.620 --> 00:07:32.309
across, the cartels are still going to transport
their product, but they’re just going to

00:07:32.309 --> 00:07:34.919
use more advanced tactics.

00:07:34.919 --> 00:07:40.180
These tactics are going to be too costly or
difficult for smaller smugglers, but the cartels,

00:07:40.180 --> 00:07:45.620
with their vast resources, will be able to
build tunnels, use drones, or use other tactics,

00:07:45.620 --> 00:07:49.719
and, with their smaller competition unable
to transport goods, they’re also going to

00:07:49.719 --> 00:07:51.300
be able to raise prices.

00:07:51.300 --> 00:07:57.339
Therefore, there’s a possibility that making
smuggling harder is helping the largest cartels

00:07:57.339 --> 00:07:59.080
make more money.

00:07:59.080 --> 00:08:03.159
A related effect happens with the human side
of smuggling.

00:08:03.159 --> 00:08:07.219
Naturally, the sections of the border that
are most secured are those that are easiest

00:08:07.219 --> 00:08:12.360
to cross—those near population centers,
roads, and areas with less treacherous terrain.

00:08:12.360 --> 00:08:17.370
That means that, as the border is secured,
that pushes migrants to more and more remote

00:08:17.370 --> 00:08:22.080
areas, where the chances of injury or death
are higher and higher.

00:08:22.080 --> 00:08:27.099
One of the more common places for migrants
to now cross into the US is the Sonoran Desert.

00:08:27.099 --> 00:08:31.909
This area is incredibly desolate and harsh,
with high temperatures in the summer rarely

00:08:31.909 --> 00:08:36.650
dipping below 100 degrees Fahrenheit or 38
degrees Celsius, and winter nighttime lows

00:08:36.650 --> 00:08:38.540
dipping below freezing.

00:08:38.540 --> 00:08:44.080
The most accessible section, next to a Mexican
Federal Highway, has a pedestrian fence preventing

00:08:44.080 --> 00:08:45.080
crossing.

00:08:45.080 --> 00:08:49.890
As these data show, this is effective at pushing
migrants further to the east.

00:08:49.890 --> 00:08:53.690
After getting dropped off near the border
on the Mexican side, migrants will fairly

00:08:53.690 --> 00:08:58.350
immediately cross the border, which is usually
one of the easiest components of their trips.

00:08:58.350 --> 00:09:03.670
The closest roads in the US to the border
in this area are Highways 85 and 86, but,

00:09:03.670 --> 00:09:08.240
as there are so few roads here, these are
major chokepoints where migrants could be

00:09:08.240 --> 00:09:09.240
caught.

00:09:09.240 --> 00:09:13.760
In addition, US Customs and Border Protection
have internal checkpoints, where they question

00:09:13.760 --> 00:09:18.520
drivers and, potentially, pull them aside
for search to determine if anyone is undocumented

00:09:18.520 --> 00:09:20.690
or smuggling illegal substances.

00:09:20.690 --> 00:09:25.590
Therefore, at the very least, migrants need
to walk twenty to thirty miles to Highway

00:09:25.590 --> 00:09:29.080
86 in order to avoid the internal checkpoint.

00:09:29.080 --> 00:09:33.660
Many might go further, all the way to Interstate
8, with the end goal of making it to Phoenix

00:09:33.660 --> 00:09:34.990
or Tucson.

00:09:34.990 --> 00:09:39.150
As soon as a migrant gets to one of these
spots, it is far easier to blend into the

00:09:39.150 --> 00:09:42.970
crowd as both cities have large immigrant
populations.

00:09:42.970 --> 00:09:46.840
From there, they’ll be able to catch public
transportation to their final destination.

00:09:46.840 --> 00:09:53.340
Many, however, don’t make it that far—and
not because they are caught by border patrol.

00:09:53.340 --> 00:09:59.790
This map, showed earlier, displays the site
of every confirmed migrant death in Arizona.

00:09:59.790 --> 00:10:06.730
The true number, as these only represent those
that are found, is likely devastatingly higher.

00:10:06.730 --> 00:10:12.220
Every year, many hundreds of individuals die
on their journey to a better life.

00:10:12.220 --> 00:10:16.780
In response to the staggering death toll in
this area, different groups have initiated

00:10:16.780 --> 00:10:21.160
efforts to help those that are dehydrated,
hypothermic, or otherwise injured.

00:10:21.160 --> 00:10:25.780
The US Customs and Border Protection Agency
itself built a system of alert beacons in

00:10:25.780 --> 00:10:28.790
this desert that those in distress can activate.

00:10:28.790 --> 00:10:33.570
In response, agents are supposed to arrive
and help the individual, but, of course, in

00:10:33.570 --> 00:10:37.230
the end, they will be deported if they lack
legal status.

00:10:37.230 --> 00:10:41.350
Other, non-governmental groups have also initiated
efforts.

00:10:41.350 --> 00:10:45.970
One of the major causes of death is dehydration,
with there being essentially no natural water

00:10:45.970 --> 00:10:50.570
source in this area, so humanitarian groups
have set up water stations at spots in the

00:10:50.570 --> 00:10:53.960
desert that migrants can use if they’re
in a dire situation.

00:10:53.960 --> 00:10:58.630
Of course, both of these interventions are
controversial—humanitarian groups say that

00:10:58.630 --> 00:11:02.440
the beacons are tricking migrants into getting
themselves arrested since there’s no clear

00:11:02.440 --> 00:11:06.280
indication that it’s border patrol that
will arrive and ultimately arrest them, rather

00:11:06.280 --> 00:11:09.890
than an ambulance or a humanitarian group,
while the border patrol says that the water

00:11:09.890 --> 00:11:14.090
stations push migrants to further continue
their dangerous journey where they might perish

00:11:14.090 --> 00:11:19.260
due to another situation, such a hypothermia,
therefore putting them at further risk.

00:11:19.260 --> 00:11:23.860
At the end of the day, though, both of these
interventions deal with the downstream effect

00:11:23.860 --> 00:11:27.980
of illegal immigration, rather than the actual
source cause of it.

00:11:27.980 --> 00:11:32.700
True, complete, physical border security can
work somewhat.

00:11:32.700 --> 00:11:38.470
It certainly cannot stop illegal immigration,
but it does curb it to an extent, but it is

00:11:38.470 --> 00:11:42.860
hugely expensive and almost certainly is not
the easiest solution.

00:11:42.860 --> 00:11:47.220
The cause of illegal immigration is not that
the border isn’t secured, and it’s not

00:11:47.220 --> 00:11:51.140
even that the countries that migrants come
from are impoverished or dangerous.

00:11:51.140 --> 00:11:57.480
The cause of illegal immigration is that legal
immigration is not an option.

00:11:57.480 --> 00:12:02.240
Nobody wants to risk their life just to move
to a new country and live in fear of deportation

00:12:02.240 --> 00:12:04.070
for the rest of their life.

00:12:04.070 --> 00:12:08.180
When Americans want to move to Canada, for
example, they don’t sneak across the border,

00:12:08.180 --> 00:12:12.630
they don’t live undocumented for the rest
of their life, restraining themselves to only

00:12:12.630 --> 00:12:13.940
certain, low-paid jobs.

00:12:13.940 --> 00:12:18.450
They take the easier route, the legal route,
because they can.

00:12:18.450 --> 00:12:22.620
If they are denied, it is generally due to
certain qualifications they lack, so they

00:12:22.620 --> 00:12:25.290
go off and gain those qualifications.

00:12:25.290 --> 00:12:30.050
Many people in Mexico, Central America, and
other less-wealthy areas, meanwhile, simply

00:12:30.050 --> 00:12:34.600
don’t even have the option of migrating
to the US legally.

00:12:34.600 --> 00:12:39.190
There is no reasonable, legal path like Americans
have for Canada.

00:12:39.190 --> 00:12:43.920
Canada, a country who’s immigration system
certainly is not perfect, has essentially

00:12:43.920 --> 00:12:50.010
halted illegal immigration, despite bordering
a country home to tens of millions of undocumented

00:12:50.010 --> 00:12:51.010
migrants.

00:12:51.010 --> 00:12:54.770
They certainly don’t let everyone who wants
to live there indefinitely do so, but they

00:12:54.770 --> 00:13:00.280
at least have a reasonable, feasible path
to legal status for anyone no matter their

00:13:00.280 --> 00:13:03.470
country of origin or economic situation.

00:13:03.470 --> 00:13:09.100
When they can go through the safe, legal route,
there’s no good reason for people to migrate

00:13:09.100 --> 00:13:10.370
illegally.

00:13:10.370 --> 00:13:15.080
Building physical systems to stop illegal
immigration might make sense if it were a

00:13:15.080 --> 00:13:17.910
physical problem, but it’s not.

00:13:17.910 --> 00:13:22.500
People don’t cross the border outside official
channels because it’s physically possible.

00:13:22.500 --> 00:13:27.340
That’s a pull factor, but illegal immigration
is caused by push factors.

00:13:27.340 --> 00:13:33.170
It’s a social problem, so to solve it, and
cut it down, you need social systems, such

00:13:33.170 --> 00:13:41.990
as a real immigration system that gives opportunities
to all, not just those that are lucky.

